EXPERIMENT 50 – COMPOUND INTEREST CALCULATION USING JUNIT PARAMETERIZED TESTS
AIM
To write JUnit test cases to calculate compound interest and validate the output using JUnit parameterized tests.
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 junit-jupiter dependency.
2. Create the CompoundInterest class with the formula:
   
   \[
   CI=P(1+R/100)^N-P
   \]
3. Throw an exception if the principal is negative.
4. Create CompoundInterestTest using @ParameterizedTest and @CsvSource.
5. Provide principal, rate, years and expected interest values.
6. Use assertEquals() with a delta of 0.01.
7. Add a test for negative principal using assertThrows().
8. Run using mvn test.
CODE
1. CompoundInterest.java
public class CompoundInterest {
    public static double interest(
        double principal, double rate, int years) {

        if (principal < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Principal cannot be negative");
        }

        return principal *
            Math.pow(1 + rate / 100, years) - principal;
    }
}


2. CompoundInterestTest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class CompoundInterestTest {

    @ParameterizedTest(
        name = "P={0}, R={1}%, N={2} -> CI={3}")
    @CsvSource({
        "1000,10,1,100.00",
        "1000,10,2,210.00",
        "5000,8,3,1298.56",
        "2000,5,0,0.00"
    })
    void computesInterest(
        double p, double r, int n, double expected) {

        assertEquals(expected,
            CompoundInterest.interest(p, r, n), 0.01);
    }

    @Test
    void negativePrincipalThrows() {
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
            () -> CompoundInterest.interest(-1000, 10, 2));
    }
}


